Title: Diagnosis of test `org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtilsEqualsIndexOfTest.testEquals()`
Details:
- The test `testEquals()` declares CharSequence variables `fooCs`, `barCs`, `foobarCs` initialized from constants presumably named `FOO`, `BAR`, `FOOBAR` respectively.
- One of the failing assertions is `assertTrue(StringUtils.equals(fooCs, (CharSequence) new StringBuilder(FOO)))`.
- The method under test is `StringUtils.equals(CharSequence, CharSequence)`.
- Inspecting the `StringUtils.equals` implementation:
  ```java
  public static boolean equals(CharSequence cs1, CharSequence cs2) {
      if (cs1 == cs2) {
          return true;
      }
      if (cs1 == null || cs2 == null) {
          return false;
      }
      return cs1.equals(cs2);
  }
  ```
- The code first checks reference equality, then handles nulls, and finally falls back to calling `cs1.equals(cs2)`.
- Since `cs1` is of type `CharSequence`, the actual `equals` invoked is the one declared on the runtime class of `cs1`.
- In the test, `fooCs` is presumably just a String (a CharSequence implementation), but the second argument is `(CharSequence) new StringBuilder(FOO)`.
- `StringBuilder` does not override `equals` from `Object`, so `StringBuilder.equals` is the default object equality, which means the comparison returns false unless they are the same object.
- Therefore, the call `StringUtils.equals(fooCs, (CharSequence) new StringBuilder(FOO))` returns false, failing the test assertion but the test expects true.
- The issue arises because the equality check is delegated to the `equals` method of the first argument, and `StringBuilder.equals()` is not overridden to compare content.
- Consequently, using `equals` here is inappropriate for cross-type CharSequence content equality.

The root cause: The method `StringUtils.equals(CharSequence, CharSequence)` relies on `CharSequence.equals()`, which is not guaranteed to compare the content of different CharSequence implementations correctly (notably `StringBuilder`).

Suggested methods where the fix should be applied:
- `org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils.equals(java.lang.CharSequence,java.lang.CharSequence)`

